 ## Overview
-Progressive Inpainting-and-Erasing (PERF) is a training strategy for single-view NeRF that avoids geometry conflicts between visible and invisible regions by computing a mask of conflicted regions and eliminating them from NeRF training [S1]. It sets itself apart from standard single-view NeRF methods by integrating a collaborative RGBD inpainting step with a progressive erasing mechanism to handle the ambiguity of unseen geometry, building on the [NeRF Training Loop](970-nerf-training-loop.md) by dynamically expanding the reference view set [S1].
+Progressive Inpainting-and-Erasing (PERF) is a training procedure for expanding a single-view Neural Radiance Field (NeRF) into a complete 3D representation by iteratively synthesizing novel views and refining the geometry. It solves the problem of geometry conflicts that arise when inpainting invisible regions with external generative models, which may violate the consistency constraints of the already-learned visible geometry [S1]. Unlike standard NeRF training which assumes multi-view consistency, PERF explicitly detects and erases conflicting regions from the supervision loss, allowing the model to converge to a coherent 3D structure from a single input [S1].
 
 ## How it works
-**Inputs and Outputs:**
-*   **Input:** A single input panorama image $I_{rgb}$ [S1].
-*   **Output:** A panoramic NeRF function $f_{nerf}$ [S1].
+The method operates on a pre-trained single-view NeRF $f_{\text{nerf}}$ initialized from a single input panorama. It uses a hash-grid based representation (Instant-NGP) and iteratively samples new camera poses, inpaints the resulting views, detects geometric inconsistencies, and fine-tunes the NeRF [S1].
 
-**Data Structures:**
-*   **NeRF Representation:** Instant-NGP hash grid [S1]. The hash table size is $2^{19}$ with 16 levels of feature vectors of channel size 2 [S1].
-*   **Reference Views Set ($I_{ref}$):** A list containing RGBD images (RGB + Depth) [S1].
-*   **Trajectories ($\{p_k\}$):** A set of novel view camera poses generated based on the initial depth map [S1].
+### Inputs and Outputs
+*   **Input:** A single RGB panorama image $I_{\text{rgb}}$.
+*   **Output:** A panoramic NeRF $f_{\text{nerf}}$ representing the complete 3D scene.
 
-**Formulas:**
-*   **NeRF Rendering:** The expected pixel color $\hat{C}(r)$ for a ray $r(t) = o + td$ is computed as [S1]:
-    $$ \hat{C}(r) = \sum_{i=1}^{N} w_i c_i $$
-    where $w_i = T_i (1 - \exp(-\sigma_i \delta_i))$, $T_i = \exp(-\sum_{j=1}^{i-1} \sigma_j \delta_j)$, and $\delta_i = t_i - t_{i-1}$ [S1].
-*   **Initial Depth Estimation:** The initial depth map $I_d$ is estimated from $I_{rgb}$ using Eq. (2) in [S1] to obtain $I_{rgbd}$ [S1].
-*   **Single-View NeRF Training:** The initial NeRF $f_{nerf}$ is trained using $I_d$ and $I_{rgb}$ based on Eq. (3) in [S1].
-*   **Invisible Regions:** Computed according to Eq. (4) in [S1].
-*   **Collaborative RGBD Inpainting:** Performed using Stable Diffusion for RGB and a depth estimator for stitching, based on Eq. (5) in [S1].
-*   **Conflicted Regions:** Computed for all $I_{ref} \in I_{ref}$ according to Eq. (6) in [S1].
+### Data Structures
+*   **NeRF Model ($f_{\text{nerf}}$):** An Instant-NGP implementation.
+    *   **Hash Table:** Size $2^{19}$, 16 levels, feature vector channel size 2 [S1].
+    *   **Parameters:** $\Theta$ (weights of the MLP and hash grid).
+*   **Reference Views Set ($I_{\text{ref}}$):** A set of RGB-D images. Initially contains the estimated depth map of the input panorama.
+*   **Trajectory Set ($\{p_k\}$):** A set of candidate novel camera poses generated based on the initial depth map, with positions gradually increasing in distance from the original view [S1].
 
